{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 9, :comments ["<b>And let them not attend to words of falsehood.</b> Let them not constantly speak words of breath, saying, Let us go sacrifice. Similar to it: ״וְאֶשְׁעָה בְחֻקֶּיךָ תָמִיד״ (Psalms 119); ״לְמָשָׁל וְלִשְׁנִינָה״ (Deuteronomy 28) — they translate it וּלְשׁוֹעֵי; וַיְסַפֵּר — וְאִשְׁתָּעִי. But it is impossible to say <b>ישעו</b> in the sense of ״וַיִּשַׁע YHWH אֶל הֶבֶל,״ ״וְאֶל קַיִן וְאֶל מִנְחָתוֹ לֹא שָׁעָה״ (Genesis 4), and to interpret אַל יִשְׁעוּ as 'let them not turn' — for if so it should have written וְאַל יִשְׁעוּ אֶל דִּבְרֵי שָׁקֶר or לְדִבְרֵי שָׁקֶר, for so is the formation of them all: ״יִשְׁעֶה הָאָדָם עַל עֹשֵׂהוּ״ (Isaiah 17), ״וְלֹא יִשְׁעֶה אֶל הַמִּזְבְּחוֹת״ (ibid.), ״וְלֹא שָׁעוּ עַל קְדוֹשׁ יִשְׂרָאֵל״ (ibid. 31) — and I have not found the usage of bet attached after them. But after a word of speech — one engaged in speaking about a matter — the usage of bet applies, such as: ״הַנִּדְבָּרִים בָּךְ״ (Ezekiel 33), ״וַתְּדַבֵּר מִרְיָם וְאַהֲרֹן בְּמֹשֶׁה״ (Numbers 12), ״הַמַּלְאָךְ הַדּוֹבֵר בִּי״ (Zechariah 4), ״לְדַבֵּר בָּם״ (Deuteronomy 11), ״וַאֲדַבְּרָה בְעֵדֹתֶיךָ״ (Psalms 119). So too here — <b>אל ישעו בדברי שקר</b> — let them not be spoken about in words of vanity and falsehood."], :book :exodus, :voice :rashi, :lang :en, :chapter 5, :notes {}, :source-witnesses nil, :timestamp "2026-08-02T08:50:57.192866440Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}